    <body>
      <sec id="article-29856.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p content-type="pubmed-excerpt">Tacrolimus is a medication used in the prevention and treatment of solid-organ transplant rejection and remains a cornerstone of modern immunosuppressive therapy. This activity focuses on the indications, mechanism of action, and contraindications of tacrolimus as a valuable agent in immunosuppressive therapy. The activity reviews the FDA-issued box warnings, adverse event profile, toxicity, and key considerations such as dosing, monitoring, and relevant drug interactions. Understanding the intricate pharmacology of tacrolimus empowers healthcare professionals to personalize treatment plans for individual patients. Comprehensive knowledge of tacrolimus equips healthcare professionals with a scientific foundation for the practical management of transplant immunosuppression and related conditions, supporting informed decision-making, optimized dosing regimens, and minimizing adverse reactions while maintaining therapeutic effectiveness.</p>
        <p>The participant learns how to apply evidence-based knowledge of tacrolimus to real-world clinical decision-making, with emphasis on safe administration, therapeutic drug monitoring, and early recognition of toxicity. This activity fosters collaboration and equips healthcare professionals with essential knowledge and tools for excellent patient care during tacrolimus administration, thereby advancing patient outcomes and care standards. The emphasis is on empowering healthcare professionals and highlighting the essential role of the interprofessional healthcare team in managing tacrolimus therapy for transplant and autoimmune patients. Collaboration among pharmacists, clinicians, and other specialists enhances patient outcomes by improving communication, coordinating monitoring strategies, reducing medication errors, and ensuring individualized, patient-centered care that supports long-term treatment success.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the medical indications for tacrolimus use.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Asess the potential adverse effects of tacrolimus.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Determine the appropriate monitoring parameters for efficacy and toxicity of tacrolimus.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement effective collaboration and communication among interprofessional team members to improve outcomes and treatment efficacy for patients who might benefit from tacrolimus therapy.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=29856&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=29856">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29856.s2" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>Tacrolimus is an immunosuppressant medication used to prevent organ rejection following transplantation. It is typically administered in combination with one or, most commonly, two other immunosuppressive agents. Tacrolimus also has a role in the prevention and treatment of certain autoimmune diseases.</p>
        <p>
<bold>FDA-Approved Indications</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>In solid organ transplantation, Tacrolimus is used to treat organ rejection in kidney, liver, and heart allografts.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tacrolimus is also now FDA-approved for the prevention of rejection in lung transplant patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Tacrolimus reduces chronic lung allograft dysfunction (CLAD).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r4">[4]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Off-Label Uses</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Graft-versus-host disease (GVHD)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r5">[5]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rheumatoid arthritis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r6">[6]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Myasthenia gravis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r7">[7]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ulcerative colitis: In mild to moderate proctitis that is unresponsive to topical 5-ASA, the American College of Gastroenterology recommends using tacrolimus/beclomethasone suppositories instead of no treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r8">[8]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Topical formulation</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>FDA-approved Indication</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Tacrolimus is FDA-approved for short-term and noncontinuous chronic treatment of moderate to severe atopic dermatitis in nonimmunocompromised adults and children who have failed to respond adequately to other topical prescription treatments for atopic dermatitis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>According to guidelines by the&#x000a0;2023 American Academy of Allergy, Asthma and Immunology/American College of Allergy, Asthma and Immunology, topical calcineurin inhibitors are important topical therapies for atopic dermatitis. In patients aged 3 months or older with uncontrolled atopic dermatitis refractory to moisturization alone, the panel recommends adding a topical calcineurin inhibitor such as pimecrolimus or tacrolimus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Off-label Uses</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Oral lichen planus&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r11">[11]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pyorderma gangrenosum&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r12">[12]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Vitiligo&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r13">[13]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Crohn disease&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r15">[15]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29856.s3" sec-type="Mechanism of Action">
        <title>Mechanism of Action</title>
        <p>Tacrolimus (FK 506) is a potent macrolide lactone immunosuppressive drug. Tacrolimus is a calcineurin inhibitor. It inhibits T-cell proliferation by binding to FK506 binding protein (FKBP).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Like cyclosporine, tacrolimus inhibits T-cell activation by blocking calcineurin. Tacrolimus binds to an intracellular protein, FKBP-12, an immunophilin structurally similar to cyclophilin. A complex forms comprising tacrolimus&#x02013;FKBP-12, Ca<sup>2+</sup>, calmodulin, and calcineurin, leading to inhibition of calcineurin phosphatase activity. This inhibition prevents the dephosphorylation and nuclear translocation of nuclear factor of activated T-cells, thereby reducing T-cell activation. Thus, although the intracellular receptors differ, cyclosporine and tacrolimus both target the same immunosuppressive pathway.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Pharmacokinetics</bold>
</p>
        <p>The following are pharmacologic parameters of tacrolimus:</p>
        <p>
<bold>Absorption</bold>: Tacrolimus oral bioavailability is incomplete and variable, generally ranging from 17% to 23% depending on transplant type and patient population. Immediate-release capsules require twice-daily dosing and demonstrate variable absorption influenced by food intake; high-fat meals markedly reduce peak concentrations and overall exposure. Extended-release capsules provide more consistent absorption with once-daily dosing and a longer half-life. Granule formulations used in pediatrics have slightly higher bioavailability compared to&#x000a0;immediate-release&#x000a0;capsules, necessitating therapeutic drug monitoring during formulation changes. Topical tacrolimus shows minimal systemic absorption, with blood concentrations often below quantifiable limits, limiting systemic exposure and toxicity risk.&#x000a0;P-glycoprotein (P-gp/ABCB1), an efflux transporter in the gastrointestinal tract, actively pumps tacrolimus back into the intestinal lumen, limiting its absorption and contributing to interpatient variability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Distribution</bold>: Tacrolimus is extensively bound (~99%) to plasma proteins, primarily albumin and alpha-1-acid glycoprotein, and shows high affinity for erythrocytes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;The volume of distribution is large (~30 L/kg), reflecting widespread tissue distribution. These distribution characteristics are consistent across oral formulations. Topical tacrolimus achieves high local tissue concentrations with negligible systemic distribution.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Metabolism</bold>: Metabolism is primarily hepatic via the CYP3A4 and CYP3A5 isoenzymes, resulting in demethylation and hydroxylation to multiple metabolites, including the major 13-demethyl tacrolimus. Some metabolites retain immunosuppressive activity. Tacrolimus also acts as a CYP3A4 inhibitor, which may contribute to clinically significant drug interactions. Metabolic pathways do not significantly differ between formulations.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Elimination</bold>: Tacrolimus is predominantly eliminated via biliary excretion into feces (~93%), with less than 1% excreted unchanged in urine. Clearance varies with age and clinical status, averaging around 30 mL/min/kg and generally higher in pediatric populations. Half-life varies by formulation and patient characteristics:&#x000a0;immediate-release&#x000a0;formulations exhibit variable half-lives in adults (approximately 2.1 to 46 hours) with prolongation in hepatic impairment; extended-release formulations have a mean half-life of approximately 38 hours; pediatric patients show shorter half-lives (approximately 10 to 12 hours). Topical tacrolimus has minimal systemic clearance due to negligible systemic absorption.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>In summary, salient features are described below</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Metabolism: CYP3A4, CYP3A5, P-glycoprotein (PGP)/ABCB1</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Metabolites: Fifteen possible; chief breakdown metabolite: 13-<italic toggle="yes">O</italic>-dimethyl-tacrolimus</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Enzymes inhibited: CYP3A4</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Half-life: 4 to 41 h (about 12 h on average)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The volume of distribution (using plasma separated at 37&#x000b0;C): approximately 30 L/kg</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Clearance (using plasma separated at 37&#x000b0;C): 1.69 +/- 0.57 L/h/kg</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Excretion: Biliary route (approximately 95%), urinary excretion (approximately 2.4% as unchanged drug)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29856.s4" sec-type="Administration">
        <title>Administration</title>
        <p>
<bold>Available Dosage Forms and Strengths</bold>
</p>
        <p>Tacrolimus can be administered orally, sublingually, topically, or intravenously. Oral tacrolimus is available in immediate-release and extended-release formulations (capsule and tablet). Tacrolimus is available in immediate-release capsules in 0.5 mg, 1 mg, and 5 mg strengths. Extended-release formulations include capsules in 0.5 mg, 1 mg, and 5 mg strengths, and tablets in 0.75 mg, 1 mg, and 4 mg strengths. The injectable solution is supplied at a concentration of 5 mg/mL. Additionally, granules for oral suspension are available in 0.2 mg and 1 mg packets. The various formulations have different pharmacokinetic parameters and are not interchangeable. Doses should be titrated to target trough concentrations.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Prevention of Post-Organ Transplant Rejection (Adult Dosing)</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Liver Transplant:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Oral:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Immediate-release: Initially 0.1 to 0.15 mg/kg/day in&#x000a0;2 divided doses in combination</p></list-item><list-item><p>Extended-release: 0.1 to 0.2 mg/kg once daily in combination with a corticosteroid. Please note that the&#x000a0;capsule formulation is not approved for liver transplants in the United States due to increased mortality in liver transplants in women receiving the&#x000a0;capsule formulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r25">[25]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intravenous:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Initially, 0.03 to 0.05 mg/kg/day as a continuous infusion.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Heart Transplant: </bold>In combination with an antimetabolite</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Oral:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Immediate-release: Initially 0.075 mg/kg/day in&#x000a0;2 divided doses, given every 12 hours.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intravenous:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Initially, 0.01 mg/kg/day as a continuous infusion.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Kidney Transplant:&#x000a0;</bold>Use in combination with an antimetabolite agent</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Oral:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Immediate-release: Initially, 0.2 mg/kg/day in combination with azathioprine or 0.1 mg/kg/day combined with mycophenolate mofetil.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Extended-release:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Capsule: 0.15 to 0.2 mg/kg/day with basiliximab induction; 0.2mg/kg/day without basiliximab induction.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Tablet: Initially 0.14 mg/kg/day.</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intravenous:&#x000a0;
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>0.03 to 0.05 mg/kg/day as a continuous infusion</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Lung transplant</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Oral:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Tacrolimus with azathioprine, mycophenolate mofetil, 0.075 mg/kg/day, divided in&#x000a0;2 doses, administered every 12 hours.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intravenous:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The recommended starting dose of tacrolimus injection is 0.01-0.03 mg/kg/day in lung transplant.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Intravenous tacrolimus use is not common due to increased nephrotoxic adverse effects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Tacrolimus can be administered with or without food, but may occur with food in the presence of&#x000a0;gastrointestinal intolerance.&#x000a0;Immediate-release doses should be 12 hours apart. Dose rounding should be to&#x000a0;a whole number that is feasible with the available strengths. For example, IR tacrolimus comes in 0.5 mg, 1mg, and 5 mg strengths.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>When converting from&#x000a0;immediate-release to extended-release formulations, utilize the following factors:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Immediate-release to capsule: 1 to 1</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Immediate-release to tablet: 1 to 0.8&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r29">[29]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Sublingual-to-oral conversion rates have ranged from 1:1 to 3:1, but 1:2 has recently been the most commonly reported in studies. There has been no optimally established dosing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Specific Patient Populations</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hepatic Impairment:</bold> Tacrolimus clearance is substantially reduced in patients with severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh &#x0003e;10). These patients often exhibit higher whole-blood trough concentrations and are at increased risk for nephrotoxicity. Close therapeutic drug monitoring is essential, and dose reductions below the standard targeted range are frequently required. Posttransplant hepatic dysfunction in liver recipients may further elevate tacrolimus levels; adjust dosing based on troughs and clinical status.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Renal Impairment:</bold> Although tacrolimus pharmacokinetics in renal impairment resemble those of individuals with normal renal function, clinical practice favors initiating therapy at the lower end of the therapeutic range, especially in liver or heart transplant recipients with preexisting renal dysfunction. Additional dose reductions may be required depending on trough levels and evolving renal parameters.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pregnancy Considerations:</bold>&#x000a0;The&#x000a0;currently available data do not suggest an increased risk of major congenital malformations following in utero exposure to tacrolimus. An increased risk of low birth weight and preterm birth was reported; however, it is probably related to the maternal condition and not to the medication. Owing to reports of hyperkalemia and renal impairment in exposed infants, renal function and potassium levels should be monitored in such newborns.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;Disease-related risks are also elevated in pregnant transplant recipients, including higher rates of hypertension, diabetes, preeclampsia, and cholestasis of pregnancy. Maternal blood glucose and blood pressure should be closely monitored.&#x000a0;Healthcare professionals&#x000a0;are encouraged to advise their patients to register by contacting the Transplantation Pregnancy Registry International at 1-877-955-6877 or&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.transplantpregnancyregistry.org/">https://www.transplantpregnancyregistry.org</ext-link></p>
        <p>
<bold>Breastfeeding Considerations:</bold>&#x000a0;Tacrolimus is present in breastmilk in trace amounts, and available evidence indicates that infant exposure is low. Clinical experience in transplant recipients has shown that breastfeeding while taking tacrolimus is acceptable. Exclusively breastfed infants may be monitored as clinically indicated. Serum tacrolimus concentrations may be measured if there is a concern for toxicity or if the infant shows unexpected symptoms. Topical tacrolimus results in minimal systemic absorption, and measured maternal blood concentrations are usually less than&#x000a0;2 micrograms per liter. The primary precaution is to ensure that the infant&#x02019;s skin does not come into direct contact with treated areas. Current guidance allows the use of topical tacrolimus on the nipples immediately after nursing. The nipples should be gently cleaned before the next feeding. Only water miscible preparations, such as creams or gels, should be applied to the breast or nipple. Ointments should be avoided because they may expose the infant to mineral paraffins through licking. When therapy of the nipple area is needed, pimecrolimus may be preferred because it is available in water-miscible formulations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;The decision to breastfeed requires balancing the benefits of breastfeeding, the mother's clinical need for tacrolimus, and potential risks to the infant.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pediatric Patients:</bold> Safety and effectiveness are established for pediatric liver, kidney, heart, and lung transplant recipients. Pediatric patients often require higher doses than adults to achieve similar trough concentrations. Evidence includes controlled and uncontrolled studies using immediate-release capsules and granules. Transplant registry data support use in pediatric lung transplantation when combined with mycophenolate mofetil or azathioprine.</p>
        <p>
<bold> Older Patients:</bold> Available evidence suggests similar efficacy and safety profiles to those of younger adults. Dose selection in older patients should be conservative, reflecting greater prevalence of hepatic, renal, and cardiac impairment and polypharmacy.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29856.s5" sec-type="Adverse Effects">
        <title>Adverse Effects</title>
        <p>
<bold>Adverse effects include, but are not limited to:</bold>
<bold>
</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Cardiovascular: angina pectoris, cardiac arrhythmias, hypertension</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Central nervous system: abnormal dreams, headaches, insomnia, tremors</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Dermatologic: acne vulgaris, alopecia, pruritus, rash</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Endocrine and metabolic: decreased serum bicarbonate, decreased serum iron, hypercalcemia, hyperkalemia, hyperlipidemia, hyperphosphatemia, hypertriglyceridemia, hyperuricemia, hypervolemia, hypocalcemia, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hyponatremia, hypophosphatemia, metabolic acidosis, weight gain,&#x000a0;new-onset diabetes mellitus after transplant&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r34">[34]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gastrointestinal: abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Genitourinary: urinary tract infection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hepatic: abnormal hepatic function tests</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Infection: bacterial infection, BK virus, candidiasis, cytomegalovirus, Epstein-Barr infection, herpes simplex infection, herpes zoster infection, other opportunistic infection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Neuromuscular and skeletal: arthralgia, muscle cramps</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ophthalmic: blurred vision, visual disturbance</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Otic: otalgia, otitis media, tinnitus</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Renal: acute kidney injury, increased blood urea nitrogen, increased serum creatinine, renal failure syndrome, renal tubular necrosis, decreased glomerular filtration rate, nephrotoxicity&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r36">[36]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Infection may be secondary to immunosuppression, highlighting the importance of reducing target doses while carefully monitoring to balance the risk of rejection.&#x000a0;A meta-analysis indicates that tacrolimus probably reduces the risks of acute rejection and chronic dysfunction in lung transplant recipients, without increasing survival rates. However, it may elevate the risk of developing diabetes mellitus and renal dysfunction. These findings help guide the decision between tacrolimus and cyclosporine, weighing the benefits against potential risks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Drug-Drug Interactions</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Mycophenolic acid (MPA): </bold>The co-administration of tacrolimus with mycophenolic acid products results in higher MPA exposure because tacrolimus does not interrupt enterohepatic recirculation the way cyclosporine does. Clinicians should monitor for MPA-associated adverse reactions and reduce the MPA dose when appropriate.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Grapefruit and grapefruit juice: </bold>The concurrent administration increases tacrolimus trough concentrations and increases the risk of severe toxicities, including neurotoxicity and QT prolongation. Patients should avoid grapefruit products.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Strong CYP3A inducers:</bold> The concomitant administration of strong CYP3A4 inducers such as rifampin, rifabutin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, and St John&#x02019;s wort significantly lowers tacrolimus concentrations, increasing the likelihood of rejection. When these agents are necessary,&#x000a0;clinicians should increase the tacrolimus dose and closely monitor trough levels.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Strong CYP3A inhibitors:</bold> The concomitant administration of strong CYP3A4 inhibitors, such as ritonavir and other protease inhibitors, azole antifungals such as voriconazole and posaconazole, macrolide antibiotics such as clarithromycin, nefazodone, and letermovir, markedly elevates tacrolimus concentrations, sometimes producing rapid, clinically significant peaks. Clinicians should reduce the tacrolimus dose when co-administered with voriconazole or posaconazole, and initiate early, frequent monitoring within the first several days of co-administration.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Mild to moderate CYP3A inhibitors: </bold>Patients receiving CYP3A inhibitors, including erythromycin, fluconazole, clotrimazole, calcium channel blockers such as diltiazem and verapamil, amiodarone, danazol, ethinyl estradiol, cimetidine, and proton pump inhibitors, also increase tacrolimus levels.&#x000a0;Clinicians&#x000a0;should monitor trough concentrations and adjust the tacrolimus dose as needed. Other interacting drugs, such as magnesium&#x02013;aluminum hydroxide antacids and metoclopramide, can increase tacrolimus exposure. Clinicians should monitor trough levels and reduce the dose when appropriate.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Mild to moderate CYP3A inducers: </bold>The concomitant administration of Mild to moderate CYP3A inducers, such as methylprednisolone and prednisone, may decrease tacrolimus concentrations. Dose adjustments should be guided by trough level monitoring. Caspofungin decreases tacrolimus trough concentrations, and therapy should be accompanied by monitoring and titration of the tacrolimus dose. Direct-acting antiviral treatment for hepatitis C can alter tacrolimus exposure because metabolic clearance changes with improvement in hepatic function. Clinicians should closely monitor tacrolimus levels during DAA treatment and adjust dosing to maintain therapeutic exposure. Cannabidiol increases tacrolimus concentrations, requiring close monitoring and potential dose reduction to avoid toxicity.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Caspofungin</bold>: The concomitant administration of caspofungin with tacrolimus can decrease tacrolimus whole blood trough concentrations. Monitor tacrolimus whole-blood trough concentrations and adjust the tacrolimus dose as required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r37">[37]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Magnesium and aluminum hydroxide antacids:</bold>&#x000a0;These antacids may increase tacrolimus whole-blood trough concentrations and increase the risk of severe adverse reactions, such as neurotoxicity and QT prolongation. Clinicians should monitor tacrolimus whole blood trough concentrations and reduce the tacrolimus&#x000a0;dose if needed.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold> Metoclopramide</bold>: The concomitant administration of metoclopramide may increase tacrolimus whole blood trough concentrations and increase the risk of serious adverse reactions, including neurotoxicity and QT prolongation. Clinicians should monitor tacrolimus whole blood trough concentrations and adjust the tacrolimus&#x000a0;dose downward as necessary.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r38">[38]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29856.s6" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>Contraindications to tacrolimus include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypersensitivity&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r39">[39]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Polyoxyl 60 hydrogenated castor oil (HCO-60) or other components of the formulation&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r40">[40]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>FDA-issued Box Warnings: Malignancies and Serious&#x000a0;Infections</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Lymphoma and other Malignancies</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Patients receiving immunosuppressants, including tacrolimus, are at increased risk of developing lymphomas and other malignancies, particularly dermatologic malignancies. The risk appears to be related to the intensity and duration of immunosuppression rather than to any specific drug. Examine patients at increased risk for skin cancer. Limit exposure to sunlight and UV light by wearing protective clothing and applying a broad-spectrum sunscreen with a high SPF.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Posttransplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD) has been observed in immunosuppressed organ transplant recipients. Most cases of Posttransplant lymphoproliferative disorder seem related to Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) infection. Monitor EBV serology during treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r41">[41]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Serious Infections</bold>
</p>
        <p>Bacterial, viral, fungal, and protozoal infections, including opportunistic infections. These infections may lead to serious, including fatal, outcomes.&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Cytomegalovirus infections: CMV-seronegative transplant recipients who receive an organ from a CMV-seropositive donor are at increased risk of developing CMV viremia and CMV disease.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>JC virus-associated progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>polyomavirus-associated nephropathy, mainly caused by BK virus infection&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r42">[42]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r43">[43]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>It is advisable to monitor for infection and adjust the immunosuppressive regimen to balance the risk of rejection with that of infection.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Precautions/Warnings</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cannabidiol</bold>: Co-administration of cannabidiol and tacrolimus may lead to higher tacrolimus blood levels, raising the risk of tacrolimus-related toxicity. Regular monitoring of tacrolimus levels and vigilance for signs of toxicity are advised. Dose adjustments of tacrolimus should be considered based on clinical judgment when used together with cannabidiol.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r44">[44]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Sirolimus</bold>: Tacrolimus is not recommended for use with sirolimus. The combination of sirolimus with tacrolimus in liver transplant patients has been linked to increased mortality, graft loss, and hepatic artery thrombosis. Similarly, using sirolimus with tacrolimus in heart transplant patients is advised against due to a higher risk of insulin-dependent posttransplant diabetes mellitus, impaired renal function, and wound healing complications. Combining sirolimus with tacrolimus may also elevate the risk of thrombotic events and microangiopathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r45">[45]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>New-onset diabetes after transplantation:</bold>&#x000a0;New-onset diabetes after transplantation has been reported. Hispanic and African-American kidney transplant patients are at higher risk.&#x000a0;Blood glucose levels should be closely monitored in patients receiving tacrolimus.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Nephrotoxicity: </bold>Tacrolimus, like other calcineurin inhibitors, can cause acute or chronic nephrotoxicity in transplant patients due to its vasoconstrictive effect on renal blood vessels, toxic tubulopathy, and tubular-interstitial effects. Acute kidney injury related to tacrolimus toxicity can lead to high serum creatinine, hyperkalemia, decreased urea secretion, and hyperuricemia, and is usually reversible. In patients with elevated serum creatinine and tacrolimus whole-blood trough levels outside the recommended range, consider reducing the dose or temporarily stopping tacrolimus. The risk of nephrotoxicity may increase when tacrolimus is given together with CYP3A inhibitors or drugs associated with nephrotoxicity (e.g., aminoglycosides, ganciclovir, amphotericin B, cisplatin, nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, protease inhibitors). When tacrolimus is used along with other known nephrotoxic drugs, monitor kidney function and tacrolimus blood concentrations closely, and adjust the doses of both tacrolimus and any concomitant medications accordingly. For more details, refer to drug interactions.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neurotoxicity</bold>: Tacrolimus may cause neurotoxicity. The most severe neurotoxicities include posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES), which is characterized by headache, seizures, visual disturbances, and confusion.Tacrolimus may also cause other neurologic issues, including delirium, tremor, paresthesia, altered mental state, and abnormalities in motor or sensory function. These effects often correlate with elevated tacrolimus whole blood trough levels. Clinicians should monitor for new or worsening neurologic symptoms and consider reducing the dose or discontinuing the medication if neurotoxicity is suspected.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r46">[46]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hyperkalemia</bold>: Hyperkalemia has been associated with tacrolimus use. It is essential to monitor serum potassium levels regularly. When using tacrolimus, consider the potential interactions with other medications that can cause hyperkalemia, such as potassium-sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitors, and angiotensin receptor blockers. Periodic testing of serum potassium should be conducted throughout the treatment.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hypertension</bold>: Hypertension&#x000a0;is an adverse effect of tacrolimus therapy&#x000a0;and may require antihypertensive treatment. Blood pressure control can be achieved with any of the common antihypertensive agents. However, careful consideration should be given before using agents that can cause hyperkalemia, such as potassium-sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitors, and angiotensin receptor blockers.&#x000a0;Calcium-channel blocking drugs may increase tacrolimus blood concentrations and, therefore, require a dosage reduction of tacrolimus.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>QT Prolongation:&#x000a0;</bold>Tacrolimus may prolong the QT/QTc interval and may cause&#x000a0;Torsades de Pointes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r47">[47]</xref>&#x000a0;Avoid using tacrolimus in patients with congenital long QT syndrome. For patients with congestive heart failure, bradyarrhythmias, or those taking certain antiarrhythmic drugs or other medications that prolong QT, as well as individuals with electrolyte imbalances such as hypokalemia, hypocalcemia, or hypomagnesemia, consider performing electrocardiograms and regularly monitoring electrolyte levels.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Immunizations</bold>: Whenever possible, administer the complete vaccine series before transplantation and before initiating tacrolimus therapy. Live vaccines should be avoided during tacrolimus treatment; this includes, but is not limited to, measles, mumps, rubella, intranasal influenza, oral polio, varicella, yellow fever, BCG, and TY21a typhoid vaccines. Inactivated vaccines deemed safe posttransplantation may not elicit a sufficient immune response during tacrolimus therapy.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Myocardial Hypertrophy</bold>: Myocardial hypertrophy has been observed in infants, children, and adults, especially in those with high tacrolimus trough levels. Myocardial hypertrophy typically presents as echocardiographic concentric thickening of the left ventricular posterior wall and interventricular septum. This condition seems to be reversible in most cases after reducing the dose or discontinuing medication.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;If myocardial hypertrophy is diagnosed, consider lowering or stopping tacrolimus.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Thrombotic Microangiopathy:</bold>&#x000a0;Cases of thrombotic microangiopathy (TMA), including hemolytic-uremic syndrome (HUS) and thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP), have been reported in patients receiving tacrolimus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r49">[49]</xref>&#x000a0;Risk factors for TMA in transplant patients include, for example, severe infections, graft-versus-host disease (GVHD), HLA mismatch, and mTOR inhibitors. These factors may, either alone or in combination, increase the risk of TMA. In patients showing signs and symptoms of TMA, consider tacrolimus as a potential risk factor. The concurrent use of tacrolimus and mTOR inhibitors may also contribute to TMA risk. A high level of suspicion is necessary to identify tacrolimus-induced TMA accurately and to provide prompt, appropriate care, including discontinuation of tacrolimus and supportive measures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r50">[50]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Pure Red Cell Aplasia: </bold>Cases of pure red cell aplasia (PRCA) have been documented in patients receiving tacrolimus. The exact mechanism behind tacrolimus-induced PRCA remains unclear. Reported cases often involved patients with risk factors such as parvovirus B19 infection, underlying health conditions, or concurrent medications. If PRCA is diagnosed, it is recommended to discontinue tacrolimus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r51">[51]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29856.s7" sec-type="Monitoring">
        <title>Monitoring</title>
        <p>Tacrolimus is a drug with a narrow therapeutic index. Therapeutic monitoring of tacrolimus in transplant patients is a valuable tool in adjusting drug levels. Since tacrolimus use is typically in combination with other immunosuppressants, target levels usually decrease as posttransplant time increases to minimize calcineurin inhibitor-mediated nephrotoxicity and adverse effects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r52">[52]</xref>&#x000a0;Whole-blood concentrations should be used, typically drawn within 30 minutes before the next dose. Therapeutic levels range from 5 to 20 mcg/mL, though 5 to 15 mcg/mL is often employed to alleviate toxicity while preventing rejection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r53">[53]</xref></p>
        <p>Additional monitoring parameters include renal function, hepatic function, serum electrolytes (Mg, Phos, K), glucose, and blood pressure. Parameters should initially be measured&#x000a0;2 to&#x000a0;3 times a week postoperatively, gradually decreasing as time passes, achieving target levels, and patient stabilization.&#x000a0;Patients receiving intravenous tacrolimus should be monitored for signs/symptoms of hypersensitivity reactions.&#x000a0;The intravenous formulations of both tacrolimus and cyclosporine contain polyoxyethylated castor oil.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The following are recommendations for tacrolimus level ranges per British Columbia Transplant Guidelines (http://www.transplant.bc.ca/health-professionals/transplant-clinical-guidelines). However, target levels vary by institution, induction protocols, and patient needs.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Adult and Kidney and Kidney/Pancreas Transplant</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Less than 1 month: 8 to 12 ng/mL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>From 1 to 3 months: 6 to 9 ng/mL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Greater than 3 months: 4 to 8 ng/mL</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Adult Liver Transplant</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Less than 1 month: 6 to 9 ng/mL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>From 1 to 3 months: 4 to 8 ng/mL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Greater than 3 months: 4 to 6 ng/mL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>More than 12 months: 3 to 5 ng/mL</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Adult Heart Transplant</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Less than 3 months: 9 to 12 ng/mL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>From 3 to 6 months: 8 to 9 ng/mL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>From 6 to 12 months: 6 to 8 ng/mL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Over 12 months: 4 to 8 ng/mL</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Adult Lung Transplant</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>From 0 to 3 months: 10 to 12 ng/mL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>From 4 to 12 months: 8 to 10 ng/mL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>More than 12 months: 6 to 8 ng/mL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Due to the metabolism pathway of tacrolimus, many drug-drug interactions exist. If starting medications that inhibit or induce the metabolism of tacrolimus, additional monitoring is suggested to prevent supra- or sub-therapeutic levels.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29856.s8" sec-type="Toxicity">
        <title>Toxicity</title>
        <p>
<bold>Signs and Symptoms of Overdose</bold>
</p>
        <p>Tacrolimus toxicity commonly presents as acute kidney injury. Close monitoring of serum creatinine, glomerular filtration rate, and urine output is necessary for patients on tacrolimus.&#x000a0;Toxicity may also present as adverse effects, including tremors, electrolyte disturbances, and headaches. Monitor for neurotoxicity, nephrotoxicity, and hypertension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r54">[54]</xref> Investigations may indicate increased serum creatinine. An&#x000a0;electrocardiogram should be obtained to rule out QT prolongation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r47">[47]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Management of Overdose</bold>
</p>
        <p>No specific antidote is currently available for tacrolimus toxicity, and the drug is not removed by hemodialysis. Management focuses on supportive care, including symptom monitoring and management. Consultation with the poison control center is recommended for current guidance and treatment recommendations.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29856.s9" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Management of posttransplant patients is inherently an interprofessional healthcare team effort. The combined expertise of each discipline contributes to graft longevity and improved patient quality of life. According to CMS guidelines, the minimum interprofessional transplant team includes: medical/surgical transplant clinicians, nursing, clinical transplant coordinators, social services, nutritional services, pharmacology, and financial coordination. Ensuring patients are educated and supported across medical, financial, dietary, and medication domains is essential to successful outcomes, with specialty nursing playing a key role in patient counseling.</p>
        <p>Posttransplant care involves close inpatient and outpatient follow-up. Nursing coordinates the initial administration of tacrolimus and collaborates with pharmacy staff to ensure accurate dosing. The pharmacy conducts medication reconciliation at baseline and as regimens change to prevent drug interactions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29856.r55">[55]</xref> Nursing also monitors for adverse effects, promptly reporting findings to the treating clinician. Clinicians integrate these observations with trough concentrations and laboratory data to guide dose adjustments. Specialty services, including transplant surgery, nephrology, hepatology, cardiology, and pulmonology, support long-term immunosuppression and graft surveillance. Neurologists evaluate tacrolimus-associated neurotoxicity, such as posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome, while nephrologists assess nephrotoxicity and adjust therapy as needed. Consistent communication among nursing, pharmacy, clinicians, and the broader transplant team ensures stable tacrolimus exposure, minimizes adverse effects, and improves patient outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29856.s10">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
